+# Passkey account integration
+
+This repository is a static site with no authentication service. The account UI uses explicitly labeled sample data when the API is absent (404/405 or an HTML static-site fallback). Sample changes persist in localStorage and never invoke authenticator APIs. API authorization, network, or malformed-data errors show an unavailable state rather than sample credentials.
+
+Implement these same-origin endpoints in your authenticated backend:
+
+- `GET /api/account/passkeys`
+- `PATCH /api/account/passkeys/:credentialId` with `{ "name": "New device name" }`
+- `DELETE /api/account/passkeys/:credentialId`
+
+Every response must return the complete, current account snapshot:
+
+```json
+{
+  "displayName": "Jamie Davis",
+  "rpId": "your-real-domain.example",
+  "userId": "base64url-WebAuthn-user-handle",
+  "csrfToken": "session-bound-token",
+  "passkeys": [
+    {
+      "id": "base64url-credential-id",
+      "name": "MacBook Pro",
+      "type": "laptop",
+      "provider": "iCloud Keychain",
+      "created": "2026-09-12",
+      "lastUsed": "Today",
+      "current": true
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Authenticate every endpoint, enforce credential ownership, validate CSRF tokens and Origin for mutations, validate trimmed names (1–50 characters), and return `Cache-Control: no-store`. Never include private key material. The current-device indicator must come from the authenticated session. Deletion must revoke the key before returning the remaining complete credential list. Consider requiring recent authentication and enforcing a recovery option for last-key deletion on the server. The client warns before last-key deletion.
+
+The UI calls feature-detected `PublicKeyCredential.signalAllAcceptedCredentials` on account load and after a successful mutation, using only the real server snapshot. Unsupported browsers receive manual passkey-manager guidance. Signal failures do not undo an already successful account change and can be retried via the info icon. The API reports a notification, not guaranteed physical deletion across all authenticator providers. A friendly credential rename is local account metadata; WebAuthn does not expose a per-credential rename signal.
+
+Reference: https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/#sctn-signal-methods
+
+No development or preview server is needed to check the JavaScript: `node --check app.js`.
